  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial. A simple root has a multiplicity of $1,$ while a multiple root has a multiplicity that is greater than $1.$ \n\nFrom the factored form, we can tell that the roots of $p(x)$ are $x=8,$ $x=-5,$ and $x=4.$ \n\n- Statement I is true. The factor $(x-8)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=8$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $4$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
